WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.082
A payment gateway SDK calls your listener on its own thread, once per event.

2
00:00:08.082 --> 00:00:09.711
Your code wants a Flow.

3
00:00:09.933 --> 00:00:13.011
callbackFlow bolts a mailbox to the SDK's door.

4
00:00:13.011 --> 00:00:18.314
Your listener drops each event through the slot with trySend, which never blocks.

5
00:00:18.314 --> 00:00:21.260
Your collector takes them out, one at a time.

6
00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:23.482
awaitClose is you on the porch.

7
00:00:23.482 --> 00:00:31.152
When the flow ends, by take, a cancelled collector or an error, it unscrews the box, and the listener is unregistered.

8
00:00:31.152 --> 00:00:35.183
Leave it out, and the library throws an IllegalStateException.

9
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:43.006
Now the SDK connects and replays 200 events in one burst, while your collector waits in receive.

10
00:00:43.006 --> 00:00:44.191
How many get in?

11
00:00:46.967 --> 00:00:47.200
65.

12
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.777
The first goes straight into the waiting hand.

13
00:00:50.777 --> 00:00:52.721
The next 64 fill the box.

14
00:00:52.721 --> 00:01:00.497
The other 135 trySend calls fail, and if you ignore the result, those events vanish without a sound.

15
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.865
So choose a policy on purpose.

16
00:01:02.865 --> 00:01:10.228
A buffer(500) right after callbackFlow is fused into the same box, and conflate keeps only the latest.

17
00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:18.969
Here is the bridge in Kotlin, and Reactor's Flux.create beside it, where onDispose does the job of awaitClose.

18
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.540
One more edge, in tests.

19
00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:24.586
The runTest clock jumps from task to task.

20
00:01:24.586 --> 00:01:27.415
Here a task is due at 100 milliseconds.

21
00:01:27.633 --> 00:01:32.784
advanceTimeBy(100) runs what is due before now plus 100, not at it.

22
00:01:32.784 --> 00:01:36.628
The clock stands at 100, and the task has not run.

23
00:01:36.833 --> 00:01:40.089
runCurrent runs what is due now, and the task fires.

24
00:01:40.089 --> 00:01:44.847
Reactor's virtual clock includes the end point, so there one call is enough.

25
00:01:45.767 --> 00:01:48.097
So a callbackFlow is a mailbox.

26
00:01:48.097 --> 00:01:55.913
trySend drops events in, awaitClose takes the box down, and a burst gets 64 slots plus one waiting hand.

27
00:01:56.133 --> 00:01:56.970
Quick check.

28
00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:02.201
100 events hit a default callbackFlow while the collector waits in receive.

29
00:02:02.201 --> 00:02:04.153
How many trySend calls fail?
